Google common protocol buffers
==============================

The .proto files under this directory are vendored verbatim from googleapis:

    https://github.com/googleapis/googleapis
    Copyright 2015-2025 Google LLC
    Apache-2.0 (see LICENSE)

They are the import closure of the set that `proto-google-common-protos` publishes — the same
files Maven drops into `target/protoc-dependencies/<hash>/` when a service builds. Fetch them
again with the loop in ADR 045; the set is closed under `import`, so a re-vendor that adds a
file adds whatever that file imports too.


Why they are here
-----------------

A spec that says `import "google/api/annotations.proto"` has to find that file somewhere.
preman's include dirs are the spec's own ancestors, bounded by the workspace, the checkout or
the shared link (ADRs 038 and 042). A `google/` tree is never an ancestor of a service's proto,
so the import can only resolve if preman brings one.

`google/protobuf/*` is not here and must not be added: protobufjs answers those from its own
bundled `common` map before an include dir is ever consulted, and @grpc/proto-loader registers
four more on top. A copy here would be dead weight that the loader cannot reach.

Verbatim also means unformatted: this directory is in `.prettierignore`, so re-vendoring is a
readable diff instead of a reflow.


Known upstream defect
---------------------

`google/api/service.proto` does not load, here or anywhere else. It imports both
`google/protobuf/api.proto` and `google/protobuf/type.proto`, and protobufjs's bundled copies of
those two both declare `Option`, so the second registration throws `duplicate name 'Option' in
Namespace .google.protobuf`. This reproduces with no vendored file present at all — it is not
something this directory caused or can fix. It is kept because the set is vendored closed rather
than curated, and because dropping it would only move the failure from "this file" to "the file
that imports it".
